# The netcode extension declares a name and nothing else, deliberately.
#
# Extender merges every extension's `ext.manifest` context into ONE per-build
# setting. A flag added here would land on `defold_hermes` too, which is the
# trap `defold_hermes_typed_native/ext.manifest` already documents in this
# repository. So the extension is built so that it needs no context at all:
#
#   * The vendored `netcode.c` opens libsodium with ANGLE brackets
#     (`#include <sodium.h>`), which searches only the include path. Rather than
#     declare an `includes:` context for it, `sodium.h` is vendored into this
#     extension's own `include/`, which Extender puts on the include path by
#     itself. `netcode.h` stays in `src/vendor` beside `netcode.c`, which opens
#     it with quotes.
#   * `netcode.c` and `netcode_capi.c` are C, not C++, so they do not need the
#     `-std=c++17 -fexceptions` context `defold_hermes` declares, and they do
#     not care which one is in force.
#   * `netcode_lua.cpp` is the only C++ translation unit here, and it compiles
#     under whatever C++ standard the merged context selects.
#
# Every platform is supported by compiling the same sources; there is no
# per-target library to select. The one real difference between targets is which
# transport the caller asks for - real UDP sockets on native, the host datagram
# channel on HTML5 - and that is a runtime argument, not a build setting. See
# .agents/docs/decisions/netcode-transport-per-target.md.
name: "defold_netcode"
